ct 0.235 string 2.20 states 20.7; E0 4.9128 c^2 0.4954 contact 0.234 string 2.63 states 33.1; E0 5.1577 c^2 0.4528 contact 0.179 string 3.00 states 43.8; E0 5.2178 c^2 0.4334 contact 0.628 string 2.89 states 13.1; alpha 2.608e-2 against 4.260e-3 (min overlap 0.865) | D 4: E0 4.3250 c^2 0.5537 contact 0.199 string 2.35 states 23.1; E0 4.6438 c^2 0.5110 contact 0.194 string 2.84 states 36.0; E0 4.8624 c^2 0.4603 contact 0.161 string 3.19 states 45.7; E0 4.9165 c^2 0.4718 contact 0.531 string 3.19 states 15.3; alpha 2.821e-2 against 3.688e-3 (min overlap 0.998). MEANING: inertia here is the states that must change for the whole to move. A knot (two tokens on one dock under the meeting's phase) can move only by undoing itself, so its band is flat, as a tightly bound lattice pair's is (hopping squared over the well). The clock's wrong sign is that one level is a knot and the other is not. With the knot gone the sign is right but the size is still 2.5, 6.1, 7.6 times relativity's, and the higher levels (longer strings) are heavier per unit energy than the lower: on locked tokens of mass pi / 3 against a beat, no level is light enough for inertia to equal energy (E-MTR-0024). The same reading applies to the register pair on the candidate rule (E-SPN-0206): its contact well is the knot.
